8

      ⍝ dyadic, out-of-range target: a DOMAIN ERROR, unlike monadic →N
      ⍝ (below) -- A→B's relative offset silently points somewhere
      ⍝ different every time a line is added or removed between the
      ⍝ branch and its intended target, so staying quiet about it (as
      ⍝ plain →N does, by returning) would hide exactly the kind of
      ⍝ mistake that's hardest to catch after the fact.
      ⍝
      )ERASE FOO
∇R←FOO
 R←0
 100→1
 R←R+10
∇

      FOO
DOMAIN ERROR+
FOO[2]  100→1
        ^  ^

      ⍝ monadic, out-of-range target: still just returns, unaffected --
      ⍝ →0/→N is the standard, deliberate "return" idiom and must stay
      ⍝ backward compatible; only dyadic A→B gets the stricter check
      ⍝ above.
      ⍝
      )SIC
      )ERASE FOO
∇R←FOO
 R←0
 →1000
 R←R+10
∇

      FOO
0

      ⍝ monadic, bare → as a lambda statement: still not allowed
      ⍝ (unaffected by any of the above -- only the dyadic form is
      ⍝ supported inside a lambda)
      ⍝
      { →1 }
DEFN ERROR+

      ⍝ dyadic A→B with A=0 (the "retry this statement" idiom) still
